  "image_name": "96398d171464656a6992ba0756f5e7e8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways electric charge develops at solid–liquid interfaces. Overall, it shows that surfaces become charged either because ions redistribute near a conducting object, because ionic surfactants adsorb at an interface, because crystals dissolve unevenly, or because oxide surface groups react with water.\n\nIn the upper left, a metal immersed in an electrolyte develops a potential difference: electrons remain in the metal while positive metal ions leave it and enter solution, creating charge separation at the interface. In the upper right, a layer of surfactant-like molecules sits at the liquid surface with their charged heads exposed, leaving a negatively charged interface balanced by nearby positive counterions in the liquid.\n\nThe lower left illustrates dissolution of an ionic crystal such as NaCl. Water molecules pull Na+ and Cl− away from the lattice and surround the released ions, showing hydration and ion separation in solution. The lower right depicts silica surface chemistry: surface sites first hydrolyze and then deprotonate, so at low pH the surface appears less ionized, while at higher pH it becomes more negatively charged. Together, the panels emphasize how interfacial chemistry controls electrostatic charge in aqueous systems.",
